# Grid — content in columns that collapse on their own.
#
# `Grid2` is for cards and panels (280px minimum), `Grid3` for smaller tiles
# (220px). Both are `auto-fit` + `minmax` rather than a media query, and that is
# the whole design: the columns collapse when the CONTENT runs out of room, which
# is the right trigger and keeps working inside a 350px rail as well as on a
# 350px phone. A grid governed by viewport width is a grid that is wrong in every
# container that is not the viewport.
#
# The names count columns rather than describing a purpose ("CardGrid",
# "TileGrid") because the number is the only thing a call site is actually
# choosing, and a purpose-named grid gets used for the other purpose within a
# week.
#
# THE NUMBER IN THE NAME IS A MAXIMUM AND IT IS ENFORCED. It did not used to be:
# the track minimum was the only bound, so `Grid2` meant "columns of at least
# 280px" and produced two of them in a 600px app column and three or four across a
# 1120px page measure. A site hit exactly that and widened the tracks at its own
# call site to buy the name back — which moves the width at which the name lies
# rather than fixing it. The track minimum is now `max(MIN, one-Nth-of-this-
# container)`, so the grid fits at most N however wide the container is, and still
# collapses to fewer when the content runs out of room. The rule and the arithmetic
# are in `vocabulary.fct`; a call site that wants different columns at different
# widths wants a different grid, not a wider `Grid2`.
#
#     use Grid2():
#         use Card():
#             ...
#         use Card():
#             ...
#
# A GRID OF QUERY RESULTS IS NOT WRITTEN THIS WAY, and getting it wrong is silent.
# A `for` renders as one element of its own — it is a tracked region the runtime
# re-fills when the query changes — so a `for` dropped into this slot is ONE grid
# cell containing every row, not a row per cell, and it compiles and lays out
# wrongly. The loop is the grid:
#
#     for p in Post by created desc limit 12 class "x-l-grid2":
#         use Card():
#             ...
#
# The `class` modifier is legal on any node, `for` included, so the list region
# itself becomes the grid container and each row is a direct child. This is the
# case the CSS vocabulary exists for: the rule has to be reachable without the
# wrapper, because there are containers only the runtime can render.
import "vocabulary.fct"
app FacetLayout:
    component Grid2():
        box class "x-l-grid2":
            slot
    component Grid3():
        box class "x-l-grid3":
            slot
